A||||6||fiero56||rear caliper only clamps, no release while driving???||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||09-22-2001||02:47 PM||||I replaced the rear calipers on my '88 about a month ago. Almost 2 weeks ago, a friend pointed out my driver side rear wheel was smoking. I don't know how long that had been going on, maybe since I replaced the calipers, but definitely not before.<P>Today I finally tore into it, I disconnected the parking brake thinking maybe it was dragging, but that didn't fix the problem(still smoking, still very hot). I did notice that on the inside pad where the piston makes contact with the pad there is fluid haning around, making the paint on the pad backing bubble up from fluid exposure, and the inside side-wall of the tire has streaks from fluid leakage.<P>The m/c was replaced about 1-2 months before the rear calipers were. But, the original rears were bad and did not work, so the m/c could be culprit???<P>Another note, driver side rear caliper/rotor are the hottest of all 4, followed by passenger side rear caliper/rotor, then the fronts are the coolest. This is after 1.5 mile driving with as little brake application as possible.<P>Ogre(and other informed members), I know your out there somewhere. Where should I go from here?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-22-2001||07:24 PM||||the simple fact that one is leaking means it's bad. the other may not be any better.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||09-22-2001||07:46 PM||||I plan to stop at AutoZone tommorrow and order another rebuilt pair. Lifetime warranty is nice isn't it.<P>Could this be the problem Ogre? These things seem clamped to the rotor, it is very difficult to spin the rotor with these calipers on, even with the parking brake disconnected. I will order new ones tommorrow, and see what the results are. Hopefully better than this time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||westtexas||09-22-2001||10:39 PM||||I have a similar problem on a '88 I just bought. Amazingly, the rear brakes don't drag if the ebrake is pulled tight. The only answer that sounds reasonable to me is that the actuator screws were put in on the wrong side. They may be threaded oppositely and tighten the calipers rather than loosen when you release the ebrake. I started to replace the calipers until I found out that the right rear cost $145 with the core. Your rear calipers may have overheated from the constant dragging and be cooked (just a wild guess).<P>Robert<p>[This message has been edited by westtexas (edited 09-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||09-23-2001||12:13 PM||||I just ordered another rebuilt caliper for driver side. I was told it will arrive Wed. I don't think the pad is smoking, just the fluid.<P>Rear caliper bridge in center where the pad mates with the bridge is hot on bother rears, but the bridge on the fronts is cooler. I tested the brakes this morning, and the fronts lock before the rears do. While driving, when I apply enough pressure to lock the fronts, if I push a bit more, the rears lock too, so I don't think the rears are seeing more burden than they should.<P>I just rememebered, I should put those organics on the rear too, rather than whatever the previous owner put on the rear. My brake "test" is compromised until I do that.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2farnorth||09-24-2001||06:17 AM||||I had that happen to one of mine.  It turned out to be the brake hose for that caliper.  It looked okay on the outside, but had collapsed on the inside.  Would allow fluid to pass to the caliper but not back when the brake was released.<P>Dave||||reg||2
Z||000006||fiero56||10-09-2001||10:13 PM||||Well, I put the replacement driver rear caliper on Sunday, and bled the rear circuit tonight with the help of a friend. I need to get th rest of the air out, but it is good for now. <BR>First impression is the rears are not overheating anymore. I haven't had the chance to lock up the brakes to see if the fronts still lock up first(I replaced the rear pads sunday with the same organic type I have on front). I also didn't try the parking brake yet, but will tommorrow when it is daylight so I can see if they smoke if not releasing. <P>At this point it just looks like the caliper was bad out of the box. I have bought 2 rear calipers bad out of the box since owning this car, both were for driver side rear.||||reg||1
